From: rcllf local patch
Subject: [PATCH] federate: run the DNET handler under env->mutex

handle_downstream_next_event_tag() runs on the RTI listener thread. When the
incoming DNET is below the last skipped NET it resends that NET and then
rewrites last_sent_NET and last_skipped_NET. It did so without env->mutex,
while the main thread reads and writes the same fields in
lf_send_next_event_tag() under env->mutex. Preempted between its check of
last_skipped_NET and the socket write (CPU stress, or a 20 ms sleep put there
for the falsification test), the listener sent a NET the main thread had
already superseded: the main thread had skipped further NETs, sent a newer NET
and set last_skipped_NET to NEVER, and the listener's NET (the old skipped tag,
or NEVER read after the main thread's write) reached the RTI after the newer
one. The RTI records any NET unconditionally, so the federate's next event was
recorded in its past (or as NEVER).

In a federation where the two federates are upstream of each other through
`after` delays (two hosts, delay 4999999 ns) that stale
record blocks both: the peer's earliest incoming message tag is computed from
it and stays below the peer's next event, and the federate's own grant is
bounded by its stale tag plus the cycle delay. Both main threads wait in
lf_cond_wait for a TAG that never comes; the listener's post-send write set
last_skipped_NET to NEVER, so later DNETs trigger no resend. The run ends at
the runner's SIGINT (3 of 150 stressed single-host runs, once on a board).

The fix takes env->mutex around the check, the resend and the bookkeeping, so
the handler and lf_send_next_event_tag() serialize on the same lock and the
RTI sees NETs from one federate in the order its logical time advanced. Lock
order env->mutex then lf_outbound_net_mutex is the one the main thread already
uses in send_tag(). A regression test in the colcon_lf suite
(test_fed_dnet_handler_race.py) covers it.
---
--- a/core/federated/federate.c
+++ b/core/federated/federate.c
@@ -1464,6 +1464,14 @@
 
 /**
  * Handle a downstream next event tag (DNET) message from the RTI.
+ *
+ * rcllf: the body runs under env->mutex. The main thread reads and
+ * writes last_skipped_NET, last_sent_NET and last_DNET in
+ * lf_send_next_event_tag() under that mutex. Without it this thread, preempted
+ * between its check of last_skipped_NET and the send, resent a skipped NET that
+ * the main thread had already replaced by a newer one (or by NEVER), the RTI
+ * recorded the federate's next event in its past, and the federation stalled
+ * (brake_fusion fed1host under CPU stress).
  */
 static void handle_downstream_next_event_tag() {
   size_t bytes_to_read = sizeof(instant_t) + sizeof(microstep_t);
@@ -1480,6 +1488,7 @@
 
   environment_t* env;
   _lf_get_environments(&env);
+  LF_MUTEX_LOCK(&env->mutex); // rcllf: see the comment above.
   if (lf_tag_compare(DNET, _fed.last_skipped_NET) < 0) {
     LF_PRINT_LOG("The incoming DNET " PRINTF_TAG " is earlier than the last skipped NET " PRINTF_TAG
                  ". Send the skipped NET",
@@ -1490,6 +1499,7 @@
   }
 
   _fed.last_DNET = DNET;
+  LF_MUTEX_UNLOCK(&env->mutex);
 }
 
